WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.866 --> 00:00:02.034
 All right.

00:00:02.134 --> 00:00:03.202
Gary, Eduardo,
congratulations, guys.

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.504
Your hard work has
paid off, and it

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.105
is now down to the two
of you battling it out

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:07.340
for the title of
"Forged in Fire"

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.175
Champion and that
check for $10 grand.

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:11.811
And because this
competition's all

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.580
about iconic weapons
from South America,

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:14.848
we're sticking with that theme.

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:17.117
We're giving you four
days to build this--

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:24.290
the Argentinean saber.

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:25.624
[music playing]

00:00:25.724 --> 00:00:27.493
DAVID BAKER: The Argentinean
saber was a fierce 19th century

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.729
weapon used by the
cavalry soldiers known

00:00:29.795 --> 00:00:31.664
as the Argentinean Grenadiers.

00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:34.667
These mounted warriors used the
lightweight single-edged blade

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:36.269
to deliver swift
and lethal slashes

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:40.106
against enemies, while the large
hilt protected their hands.

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:42.709
After their crucial service
in the Argentinian War

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.277
of Independence,
the Grenadiers were

00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:47.780
given the honor of becoming
the presidential escort guard.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.382
Today, these elite
soldiers still

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:52.385
protect the president with this
powerful saber at their sides.

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:53.219
 All right, guys.

00:00:53.285 --> 00:00:54.287
We will see you in four days.

00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:55.654
- Good luck.
- We got this.

00:00:55.754 --> 00:00:57.090
 Stay safe, dude.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:00.192 line:14%
[music playing]

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:01.727 line:14%
EDUARDO: My name's Eduardo Sol.

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:03.963 line:14%
I am originally from Mexico.

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:06.232
I moved to the US because
I was a foreign exchange

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:09.301
student during college and
met the woman of my dreams.

00:01:09.368 --> 00:01:11.604
And she got me
into blacksmithing.

00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:13.105
And I got hooked on knives.

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.508 line:8%
My concern is that
pipeback spine.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:16.543 line:14%
Here we go.

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:19.045 line:8%
I never knew that
existed until today.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.314 line:8%
The biggest challenge
is putting in the curve

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.316
that's the requirement
while still keeping

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:24.818
the pipeback spine intact.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:29.155
Forging is going really well.

00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:30.990
It's good enough,
it's close enough,

00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:32.592
and I am ready for the quench.

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:37.864
Ooh.

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:42.602
The blade is hard, the blade is
straight, and I am exhausted.

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:45.471
All I need to do now is
get started on my hilt.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.073
One of the parameters
is drilling

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.974
the piercings in the hilt.

00:01:49.074 --> 00:01:50.276
There's that disk.

00:01:50.342 --> 00:01:52.278
These rotary disks are
literally exploding

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:53.846
as soon as I put them on.

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:57.383 line:8%
The guard is too hard for the
rotor disks to cook through.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.253
[groans]

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.356
This one line right there took
two and a half cutting disks.

00:02:04.423 --> 00:02:07.193
I am extremely
aggravated by this.

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:10.929
This is supposed to
be quick and easy.

00:02:10.996 --> 00:02:14.234
I am burning through my day.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.434
I'm falling behind.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:18.137
I hope I'm going to
have enough time.

00:02:18.203 --> 00:02:20.373
All righty, now to
make that pretty.

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:24.310
I finally finish the
piercings on my guard.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.214
I am down to a single
rotary disk left.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.116
[laughs] But I'm
running out of time.

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:31.851
I need to fly through my handle.

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:34.520
Hopefully, everything's
going to marry up perfectly.

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:35.988
That's it.

00:02:36.054 --> 00:02:39.057
Overall, I'm still on schedule.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:41.427
After I peen this
on and close it,

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:45.030
I'm going to grind
this extra steel off

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:49.302
so it's a much tighter
fit to the handle.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:53.806
It's time to peen the guard
and then sharpen my blade.

00:02:53.872 --> 00:02:55.208
That should be it.

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:57.477
It's really coming out great.

00:02:57.544 --> 00:02:59.145
That is sharp.

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:02.949 line:14%
[music playing]

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:05.617 line:8%
GARY (VOICEOVER): I'm Gary
Graham from Elkmont, Alabama.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.321
12 days after I turned 18,
I enlisted in the Army,

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:10.322
flying UH-60 Black Hawks.

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:12.491
Started making my first
blade after I got home

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:14.760
from deployment, and
it continued to grow

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:16.328
to a small business now.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:18.430
I forged the blade out, got
the length I want out of it,

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:20.499
got it forced to shape,
drawing it out with my dies

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:22.935
and verifying that
everything's nice and even.

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:24.137
It's looking good.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:26.238
It's time to go and
sharpen the blade.

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:27.973
So I'm at the point now
I'm going to go out and do

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:29.475
some testing with my blade.

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:32.979
Whoops.

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:34.012
Missed part of it.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:35.380
I bent my blade.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.516
I come out with a blade
looking like a boomerang.

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:38.617
Damn.

00:03:38.684 --> 00:03:40.119
I only got one more
day to get it done.

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:41.453
There's no time to waste.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.356
I've got to get working
on a new blade immediately

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:45.157
and make this one work.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:47.360
We should have the
process down pat now.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:51.296
We're good on parameters.

00:03:51.396 --> 00:03:52.965
It's been a hell of a
day, but at the end,

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:54.633
I've got a quenched blade.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.869
This blade looks a lot
better than the blade

00:03:56.935 --> 00:03:58.204
that didn't work out so great.

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.373
It's time for me to work
on my hand and my hilt.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.076
I grab a piece of sheet metal,
cut out the shape that I want,

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:05.711
and I start shaping
it in the swage block.

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:08.847
Now, I'm going to cut these
piercings in in my hilt.

00:04:08.914 --> 00:04:11.651
My plan to put this handle
together to secure it properly

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:15.254
is I want to notch out my
tang, weld in a machine boat

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:17.623
with a spacer so I
can tighten it all up.

00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:19.925
And the last thing I
need to do is sharpen it

00:04:20.025 --> 00:04:21.226
and assemble the sword.

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:22.394
That's it for Round 3.

00:04:22.494 --> 00:04:25.697
It's in the judges' hands now.

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:29.601
 All right, bladesmiths,
welcome to the kill test.

00:04:29.701 --> 00:04:31.771
Well, it's time to find out just
how lethal your weapons are.

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:33.372
I'm going to take your
weapons and deliver

00:04:33.438 --> 00:04:36.242
some thrusts and slashes
on this pig carcass.

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:37.977
Gary, you're up first.
You ready for this?

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:38.944
 I'm ready.

00:04:39.045 --> 00:04:40.213
 Let's do this.

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:41.414
GARY (VOICEOVER):
Since I've already

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.615
warped one blade
during testing at home,

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:44.617
I'm nervous as a cat
on a hot tin roof.

00:04:44.683 --> 00:04:46.519
I don't want my blade to
crack, I don't want it to warp,

00:04:46.585 --> 00:04:48.086
and I don't want it to break.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:52.425
[music playing]

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:18.384
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, Gary, let's talk

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:19.785
about your weapon here.

00:05:19.885 --> 00:05:22.854
First up, most of the weight
is right in the very tip

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:24.290
of this blade.

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:26.525
But the saving grace for
that is that you have a nice

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:29.094
handle right there where
I can grab onto it,

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:31.463
I can marry my
hand, it's a void,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.900
and I've got full
control with this weapon.

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:37.536
As you can see, those
were very deep cuts.

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:41.107
Overall, sir, your weapon--

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:42.274
it will kill.

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:43.442
 Thank you.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:44.577
 All right, Eduardo,
your turn, sir.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.045
Are you ready?
- I'm excited.

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:47.046
Let's go.

00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:48.513
 Let's do this.

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:50.449
EDUARDO (VOICEOVER): I
know my edge is fantastic.

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:51.918
It can take a beating.

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:53.052
It's a killer blade.

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:56.989
I can't wait to
see what it can do.

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:58.624
 [sighs]

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:02.495
[music playing]

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:07.867
 Gentlemen, I think
we have a problem here.

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:09.936
That's a very small handle.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:11.370
My hand barely fits in there.

00:06:11.436 --> 00:06:14.006
And when I'm swinging,
there was just

00:06:14.106 --> 00:06:18.144
no retention or stop right
here, especially in the slashes.

00:06:18.244 --> 00:06:20.846
For thrusting it with
a forward weight,

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:23.149
all that is on my wrist.

00:06:23.215 --> 00:06:24.449
What do you think?

00:06:24.549 --> 00:06:25.918
BEN ABBOTT: Oh, I see
what you're saying.

00:06:25.984 --> 00:06:27.419
I could swing it now.
DOUG MARCAIDA: But swinging--

00:06:27.486 --> 00:06:28.254
 But I can't pull
it back up because I

00:06:28.354 --> 00:06:29.622
don't have the leverage.

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:32.258
 Yeah, I'm looking at
my fingers double up.

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:34.327
The problem is, is
there's no space in there.

00:06:34.427 --> 00:06:36.762
If it does cut loose,
that's breaking my hand.

00:06:36.828 --> 00:06:38.831
 Is there any way to
adjust and make it fair?

00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:41.367
 The problem with
that is I can hold back

00:06:41.433 --> 00:06:43.669
to make it work, but
now I'm holding back,

00:06:43.735 --> 00:06:45.070
and that's not an
even test anymore.

00:06:45.137 --> 00:06:46.438
 OK.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:48.107
You guys all agree, the
blade's not safe to wield?

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:49.442
 Yes.
BEN ABBOTT: Definitely.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:50.776
GRADY POWELL: OK.

00:06:50.843 --> 00:06:51.878
 [bleep]

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.015
 Eduardo, we
absolutely hate to see

00:06:56.115 --> 00:06:57.149
a competition end like this.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:59.252
But when wielding
an edged weapon,

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.888
safety is always of
the utmost importance.

00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:04.223
Due to the small
handle you've presented

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:05.791
and the lack of
retention that it offers,

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:07.693
the judges have issued
a vote of no confidence.

00:07:07.759 --> 00:07:09.795
They can't safely
test your weapon.

00:07:09.861 --> 00:07:12.331
So unfortunately, your time
in this competition has ended.

00:07:12.398 --> 00:07:13.565
I'm going to have
to ask you to please

00:07:13.665 --> 00:07:15.735
step off the "Forged" floor.

00:07:15.802 --> 00:07:16.502
 It's all right.

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:17.803
 Congratulations, buddy.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:18.604
 Thanks.

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:21.106
 Thank you though, Eduardo.

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:22.141
 I'm crushed.

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:23.442
I'm disappointed.

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:26.278
I worked really hard for
it not to get tested.

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:28.514
It fit my hand perfectly.

00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:31.684
Every step of this competition
was a learning experience.

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:34.520
And I really think I'm
leaving a better smith.

00:07:36.856 --> 00:07:39.392
 Gary, you are today's
"Forged in Fire" Champion.

00:07:39.458 --> 00:07:41.761
You got yourself a
check for $10,000.

00:07:41.828 --> 00:07:42.895
Congratulations.

00:07:42.995 --> 00:07:43.896
 Thank you.

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:45.097
I really appreciate it.

00:07:45.198 --> 00:07:46.666
It feels great to be the
"Forged in Fire" Champion.

00:07:46.766 --> 00:07:48.033
 What are you going to
do with the $10 grand?

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.869
Do you have plans?
- No, I'm sure my wife does.

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:51.203
[laughter]

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.671
GARY (VOICEOVER): I just want
to savor this for a while.

00:07:52.771 --> 00:07:54.473
 There was a lot
of work to get here.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.942
It's been fun, I've met
a lot of great people,

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.410
and I've enjoyed it.

00:07:58.511 --> 00:08:02.081
[music playing]

